
What is a DaemonSet?

A DaemonSet in Kubernetes ensures that a copy of a Pod runs on every node (or selected nodes) in the cluster.

When a new node is added → Pod is automatically created

When a node is removed → Pod is deleted automatically


Simple Definition

DaemonSet = One Pod per Node



Why Do We Use DaemonSet?

Used for node-level operations like:

Monitoring agents (Prometheus Node Exporter)

Log collectors (Fluentd, Filebeat)

Security agents

Networking plugins (CNI)



How DaemonSet Works

Scheduler ensures:

Node1 → Pod1
Node2 → Pod2
Node3 → Pod3




DaemonSet Manifest

Example 1


apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: nginx-daemonset
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: nginx-daemon
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx-daemon

  updateStrategy:
    type: RollingUpdate

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx-daemon
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80

        resources:
          requests:
            memory: "64Mi"
            cpu: "100m"
          limits:
            memory: "128Mi"
            cpu: "200m"

      # Allow DaemonSet to run on master/control-plane nodes
      tolerations:
      - key: "node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane"
        operator: "Exists"
        effect: "NoSchedule"

      # Optional: run only on specific nodes
      nodeSelector:
        kubernetes.io/os: linux



apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: node-exporter
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: node-exporter
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: node-exporter
    spec:
      hostNetwork: true
      hostPID: true

      #  Add this block so Pod can run on master
      tolerations:
      - key: "node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane"
        operator: "Exists"
        effect: "NoSchedule"

      containers:
      - name: node-exporter
        image: prom/node-exporter:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9100
          name: metrics

Verify Pods are running on all nodes including master

kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep node-exporter



Alternative Option (Not Recommended for Production)

You can remove taint from master permanently


kubectl taint nodes <master-node-name> node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule-
But prefer tolerations because:

It is clean
Works in multi-master clusters
Does not affect scheduling of other apps




